<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Capitulare de villis</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as De Villis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Capitulare de villis is a Carolingian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitulary">capitulary</a>, usually dated to around 800, setting out detailed rules for the management of royal estates. It specified the duties of stewards, the keeping of accounts and the crops, plants and animals to be raised. It is a key source on the economy and estate administration of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/capitulare-de-villis.jpg" alt="Capitulare de villis" width="276" height="401" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Charlemagne · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Capitulare_de_villis_vel_curtis_imperii_LXX.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitulary">capitulary</a> is one of several issued by Carolingian rulers on the running of their estates, and it may belong to the later years of the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>. Its origin is disputed: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> has been claimed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulda">Fulda</a> or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a>, and its date and the circumstances of its making are debated. It was probably written as the court settled at a fixed palace, to solve the problem of supplying food and equipment to an administration far from its estates.</p><p>A study of 1912 argued that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a> wrote it for his estate reform in Aquitaine in 794, citing plants that grew only in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Others have linked it to the famine of 792–3, to curbing abuses on the royal estates, or to supplying the army. No answer has won general support.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Its seventy chapters follow no logical order. Most of the text sets out the duties of the iudex, the official in charge of royal properties: to avoid corruption, guard the estates against theft, prosecute criminals fairly, present certain items to the king each year, and above all keep an accurate inventory and send the estate's food, money and goods to the court. The stewards also dispensed justice in the king's absence.</p><p>Fish ponds, byres, pigsties, sheepfolds, goat-pens, mills and barns are prescribed, with how much land to keep as forest and how much to clear, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/villa-estate">villae</a> kept ready to receive the king. Wine receives special attention, as do horses and puppies, while crops are mostly given as quotas. Craftsmen from blacksmiths to weavers were to work on each estate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>A comparison with the Brevium Exempla, which records real audits of several estates, shows that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitulary">capitulary</a> was largely aspirational and did not always, if ever, match reality. It is no longer used to explain the whole economic and social order of the Carolingian world, but remains important for its material culture and administration. Its carts and produce for the army and its care for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horse-breeding">horse breeding</a> point to preparations for war. It also shows the queen with authority over the stewards: her orders sent through the butler or seneschal were to be obeyed, and she could set a penalty when the iudex's orders went unheeded.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Capitulare de villis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The capitulary <b>De Villis</b>, generally attributed to Charlemagne's reign, describes in great detail how a royal estate was expected to function: it specified the crops to be grown, the livestock to be kept, the crafts to be practised and the accounts to be rendered to the king.</blockquote><a href="/empire/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
